s SURVIVED control (PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, no __pycache__); crash/idempotency evidence for every durable write or an explicit purity bound; a conformance matrix mapping each pinned clause to a test, a stated bound or NOT APPLICABLE with an owner. Managed Story worktree contract: the Story worktree .temp/STORY-260830-1cyj0q/worktree on branch task-board/story/STORY-260830-1cyj0q ALREADY EXISTS and its tip is the previous leaf's signed checkpoint commit — do not re-provision, rebase, commit on, amend or revert it, never edit .task-board directly, never self-accept. Your candidate is the UNCOMMITTED working tree on top of that checkpoint; the CR the runtime builds from it is the WHOLE-Story patch, so read every earlier leaf's delta (`git -C .temp/STORY-260830-1cyj0q/worktree log --stat <trunk>..HEAD`) and its outcome resources before you start. If origin/main moves before your handoff run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-32ypr2` (LOGBOOK append conflicts are resolved with --replay-resolutions: copy the retained resolution-template.json header and add {\"checkpoint_oid\": <the ORIGINAL checkpoint OID recorded before the refresh>, \"path\": \"LOGBOOK.md\", \"sha256\": <hex of the full replacement>, \"content\": <base64>} keeping every entry newest-first), then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old-base> <new-base>` that also appears in your `git status` and restore trunk content by hand — a refresh carries stale pre-refresh copies of trunk-changed files into the candidate and they read as reverts (README/LOGBOOK purely additive; task-board.config.json == HEAD; the ownership registry must MERGE the trunk rows with yours, never replace them). Before handoff `git status` shows only candidate paths. Read the Curator-managed go-testing-tools skill first; all CI local; run the full configured validation suite (30 commands as of trunk c3aae73 — STORY-260830-4qojoz added three bounded rpcwire fuzz gates; read the count from the candidate config rather than assuming it) once before handoff (command 5 is `go test ./... -race -count=1 -timeout 25m` and can take 25-40 minutes under concurrent runs — that is expected, do not shorten it; command 21 is `cataloggen -adopted … -check`). Hand off no later than 3.5 hours in — if the full scope cannot be finished, hand off a reviewable slice with the remaining items enumerated precisely in the results. Attach TASK-260830-32ypr2_results.md, TASK-260830-32ypr2_conformance-matrix.md and TASK-260830-32ypr2_producer-evidence.tar.gz (a real gzip archive), keep the checklist current, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-32ypr2 --role developer` and exit promptly. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be claude-opus-5 max.\n9|\n10|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n11|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n12|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n13|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n14|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n15|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n16|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n17|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.\n18|\n19|TRUNK STATE AT YOUR SPAWN (recorded 2026-09-18): origin/main is c3aae73 as of 2026-09-18T01:30Z and carries BOTH the v0.7.0 adoption (PR #52, commit 2fc6d50) and the mesh-RPC Story plus the resumesmoke deadline fix (PR #53 a12d1bd, PR #54 c3aae73) — internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md, internal/specpin/v0.7.0.lock.json, internal/catalog/catalog.v0.7.0.json with `catalog.Adopted = ReleaseV070`, internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json, and traceability.go pointing at that trio. Cite SPEC.v0.7.0.md with the same heading numbers as the v0.6.0 sections named above. If origin/main moves again before your handoff, run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate <this task>` and then audit every path in `git diff --name-only <old base> <new base>` that also appears in your `git status`: README.md and LOGBOOK.md moved on trunk with that landing, so keep your edits purely additive on top of the NEW content and never restore the old bytes; task-board.config.json must end byte-identical to the new base.\n20|\n21|YOUR PREVIOUS ATTEMPT FAILED AT CR CONSTRUCTION FOR A PROVENANCE REASON, NOT FOR ANYTHING IN YOUR WORK — READ THIS FIRST. The construction refused with:\n22|`delivery failure [stale-anchor]: change_request_base_authority_mismatch: the STORY-260830-1cyj0q candidate provenance disagrees: checkpoint 47294bb8 does not descend from selected authority c3aae73 while branch=47294bb8 and head=47294bb8`.\n23|Nothing about that is your content. The Story branch was cut before trunk advanced: its tip is the TASK-260830-2g5be6 checkpoint sitting on base a12d1bd, while current origin/main is c3aae73 (PR #54 landed STORY-260830-4qojoz, which also raised the configured suite from 27 to 30 commands). A Change Request must descend from the selected authority, so the branch has to move onto c3aae73 before any CR can be constructed here.\n24|So, BEFORE you do anything else: run `task-board worktree refresh-candidate TASK-260830-32ypr2`. You are the live tracked producer for this initial leaf and it provably has no Change Request, which is exactly the case that command exists for; it preserves your complete working candidate exactly and replays the two checkpoints (TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev6 and TASK-260830-2g5be6 rev3) with signing in an isolated workspace. If a regular-file conflict appears, prepare `--replay-resolutions` from the retained replay worktree's `resolution-template.json`, binding each resolution to REBASE_HEAD, its unmerged path and the SHA-256 of the explicit replacement content — keep LOGBOOK entries newest-first and never resolve by discarding trunk content.\n25|After the refresh, audit every path in `git diff --name-only a12d1bd c3aae73` that also appears in your `git status`: README.md, LOGBOOK.md and task-board.config.json all moved on trunk, so your edits must sit on top of the NEW content, never restore the old bytes, and the config must end byte-identical to the new base. Then read the command count from the candidate config rather than assuming it — it is 30 now, not 27.\n26|Everything you already wrote in the worktree is preserved and still stands: start with `git -C .temp/STORY-260830-1cyj0q/worktree status --short` and `git diff`, state in your results what you inherited, and verify it against this brief instead of redoing it. Your scope is unchanged.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
