K edits must sit on the new content without restoring old bytes. A registry that lost the sibling Story's rows is a P1 regardless of how green the suite is. Then proceed to your own independent review of the whole revision: (1) Unknown native records become raw-addressable opaque events: a record whose native type is outside the closed 26-kind registry must project to `opaque_event` with `opaque` visibility, keep a Source Evidence whose raw blob and manifest byte-range references actually resolve back to the captured bytes (sorted, unique, in range), and never be dropped, guessed, coerced into a neighbouring kind or re-typed on a second projection — project twice and compare. Drive a record that is ALMOST a known kind (right envelope, unknown inner type) as well as a wholly unknown one. (2) Foreign signed or encrypted reasoning stays opaque: it projects to `opaque_reasoning`, survives a round trip through the blob store byte-exact, and is never decrypted, re-encoded, summarized, truncated or promoted to `reasoning_summary`. Foreign instructions stay LOW-AUTHORITY history — prove an instruction carried in a foreign record cannot change the projected session's effective instruction snapshot, and that source usage never becomes target accounting. (3) Incomplete tool calls never become live actions: drive the whole matrix (call with result; call without result; result without call; a result arriving after the capture boundary; a nested or subagent call) and assert the projected kind, the visibility and the no-pending-action property for each. A mutant that lets an incomplete call project as pending or callable must redden a committed test. (4) The closed shapes underneath: Canonical Event's exact member set, zero-based contiguous ordinals, 0..64 sorted unique causal parents, the four capture statuses with a SYNTHESIZED event carrying no native Event ID and naming the core operation that made it, the closed content blocks with inline content at most 64 KiB (witness 65536