and internal/localstore.\n2|\n3|Verify with your own instruments. (0) REWORK VERIFICATION FIRST — story_final revision 3 was routed CHANGES REQUESTED by RUN-260918-d009f2 with NO P1, and its Section 0 states WITHOUT QUALIFICATION that the rev2 rework is closed AT THE CLASS: the second lenient decoder is gone from the projection path and the alias axes are pinned. Confirm that independently — grep for any second decode of the same bytes after the strict gate and re-drive the five outcomes from the rev2 verdict (foreign instruction at authority=high, unknown coerced to user_message, foreign encrypted reasoning promoted, sealed content from an unclaimed member, re-attribution to main) — but do not re-litigate what is closed. The remaining P2 was test-plus-matrix only: the AC-named invariant 'raw-reference byte ranges resolve to the captured bytes' was measured only in its DEGENERATE case (single record, offset 0), so a one-token offset-accumulation bug survived the whole suite while matrix row 4 reported the property as driven. Check the new multi-record row asserts the OFFSETS and not merely the bytes, that the labelled harness row for the offset-accumulation bug dies, and that matrix row 4 and TRACEABILITY cite it. IMPORTANT — trunk moved during this cycle: STORY-260830-ptxkqe landed as 42d5a95 and touches internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json, README.md and LOGBOOK.md, the same files this leaf owns. So verify the refresh was a MERGE and not a replacement: trunk's ptxkqe registry rows must still be present alongside this leaf's, reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256 must be re-derived from the MERGED registry (recompute it yourself and compare), the README measured-coverage subsection must equal what tracecheck prints on the merged tree and still pass the landed readme_coverage_pin_test.go, and README/LOGBOOK edits must sit on the new content without restoring old bytes. A registry that lost the sibling Story's rows is a P1 regardless of how green the suite is. Then proceed to your own independent review of the whole revision: (1) Unknown native records become raw-addressable opaque events: a record whose native type is outside the closed 26-kind registry must project to `opaque_event` with `opaque` visibility, keep a Source Evidence whose raw blob and manifest byte-range references actually resolve back to the captured bytes (sorted, unique, in range), and never be dropped, guessed, coerced into a neighbouring kind or re-typed on a second projection — project twice and compare. Drive a record that is ALMOST a known kind (right envelope, unknown inner type) as well as a wholly unknown one. (2) Foreign signed or encrypted reasoning stays opaque: it projects to `opaque_reasoning`, survives a round trip through the blob store byte-exact, and is never decrypted, re-encoded, summarized, truncated or promoted to `reasoning_summary`. Foreign instructions stay LOW-AUTHORITY history — prove an instruction carried in a foreign record cannot change the projected session's effective instruction snapshot, and that source usage never becomes target accounting. (3) Incomplete tool calls never become live actions: drive the whole matrix (call with result; call without result; result without call; a result arriving after the capture boundary; a nested or subagent call) and assert the projected kind, the visibility and the no-pending-action property for each. A mutant that lets an incomplete call project as pending or callable must redden a committed test. (4) The closed shapes underneath: Canonical Event's exact member set, zero-based contiguous ordinals, 0..64 sorted unique causal parents, the four capture statuses with a SYNTHESIZED event carrying no native Event ID and naming the core operation that made it, the closed content blocks with inline content at most 64 KiB (witness 65536 admitted and 65537 refused, and prove oversized inline content becomes a blob reference rather than a silent truncation), and the Canonical Session bounds (1..1024 actors, 1..1,000,000 ordered unique event IDs, 1..1024 heads, `parent` null only for `main`). (5) Determinism — the same captured bytes must produce byte-identical canonical output twice; and check the leaf states explicitly which parts of 13.14.2-13.14.5 this Story does NOT own, so the matrix cannot be read as a wider claim. (6) STORY-CLOSE ITEMS, the part most likely to be wrong on a final leaf: the ownership registry edited must be `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.7.0.json` (the adopted one — v0.6.0 is no longer adopted and editing it would be invisible), every binding must name a clause an executed test actually drives, `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256` must be RE-DERIVED from the registry as it stands in the candidate (recompute it yourself and compare; a digest copied from another Story or left stale is a P1), `go run ./internal/traceability/cmd/tracecheck` and the section-scoped runs must be green on the exact candidate tree with the ratios quoted verbatim in the results, the README \"Measured coverage of this repository\" subsection must equal what tracecheck prints on this tree (the landed `readme_coverage_pin_test.go` pins it — confirm the test is actually exercised and not bypassed), and the LOGBOOK entry must be newest-first and factually true. (7) Mutation — rerun the shipped harness in an isolated copy, rerun each KILLED twice, and plant at least four narrowing mutants of your own on gates the producer did not mutate (one member check dropped, one bound widened by one, an unsorted array admitted, the pre-mutation revalidation skipped) plus an applied harmless SURVIVED control; per-plant raw logs, subprocess exits, precise denominators. (8) Bounds and hygiene — the candidate must be byte-identical to trunk 2fc6d50 everywhere it is not this Story's scope (a refresh-candidate carries stale pre-refresh copies into the candidate and they read as reverts: check every path in `git diff --name-only <the CR base> 2fc6d50`), README and LOGBOOK edits pure