{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c42b","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b4ea-fb92-7cf3-9cd5-04cb5a5f5467"},"sequence":112,"recorded_at":1780531400000219,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"16aac74a-8f80-4da1-94ce-cabce45ae87b","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"16aac74a-8f80-4da1-94ce-cabce45ae87b","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"16aac74a-8f80-4da1-94ce-cabce45ae87b"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0b4eb-3038-7043-93b7-be9e1c309d1c"},"task_id":"01a0b4eb-3038-7043-93b7-be9e1c309d1c","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0b4eb-3038-7043-93b7-be9e1c309d1c","chunk":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-32ypr2/TASK-260830-32ypr2_rework-rev5.md`.\n1|THIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES EVERY EARLIER PRECONDITION ON TASK-260830-32ypr2, including TASK-260830-32ypr2_rework-rev4.md. Do exactly what is written here.\n2|Rework run for TASK-260830-32ypr2, the FINAL leaf of STORY-260830-1cyj0q. Story_final revision 4 was reviewed by RUN-260918-492d65 (claude-opus-5 max) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED with NO P1. Read TASK-260830-32ypr2_review-verdict-rev4.md first, and specifically its Section 0: the reviewer states that the ENTIRE rev3 rework is closed (P2-e raw-addressability past offset 0 CLOSED, P3-u..aa closed or declined with rationale), that the refresh onto 42d5a95 was a genuine MERGE verified row by row, that the digest was re-derived correctly, and that the README pin is live. Keep all of it. Change nothing that the verdict grades CLOSED or CONFIRMED.\n3|Trunk has NOT moved: origin/main is still 42d5a95 and your candidate is already based on it. Do not refresh, do not rebase, do not touch the registry merge the reviewer just verified row by row. Your working tree is the rev4 candidate; build revision 5 on top of it.\n4|What remains is ONE P2 and a P3 list. Every item is test-plus-matrix. The reviewer re-probed production on this exact tree and found it CORRECT at every far edge: 64 parents / 1024 heads / 1024 actors / 1,000,000 event IDs admitted, 65 / 1025 / 1025 / 1,000,001 refused. Do not change production to \"fix\" any of this. If you believe a production change is required, say so in the results with the probe that shows it and do not make it.\n5|1. P2-f, the only P2. This leaf's AC (4) names four closed-shape scale bounds -- parents 0..64, head IDs 1..1024, event IDs 1..1,000,000, actors 1..1024 -- and matrix section D reports them as \"owned by the accepted `internal/clonebundle` suite\". They are not. The reviewer widened each bound by one, twice, against the whole `internal/clonebundle` package: parents 65 build SURVIVED, parents 65 decode SURVIVED, heads 1025 decode SURVIVED, event IDs 1000001 decode SURVIVED, actors 1025 build SURVIVED. Two more (heads 1025 build, event IDs 1000001 build) died ONLY because the low-edge row's message fragment carries the bound literal -- a census kill, not a behavioural one, so they count as unwitnessed too. Seven of eight build/decode sites have no behavioural witness. This is the P2-e shape one level down: a row that reports a property as measured where the suite reaches only the trivial side. Close it: commit far-edge rows in `internal/clonebundle` for parents 65 (build AND decode), heads 1025 (build AND decode), actors 1025 (build; the decode row already exists), and event IDs 1,000,001 (build; decode as well unless you state its cost as an explicit bound WITH the seconds you measured), each ASSERTING THE REFUSAL -- the code and the message, not merely a non-nil error. Add one widening narrowing per site to `internal/clonebundle/testdata/mutant_harness.py` and show each new row kills it. Then rewrite matrix section D and `internal/clonebundle/TRACEABILITY.md` to CITE THE ROWS by name instead of delegating to \"the accepted suite\".\n6|2. P3-bb. `splitRecordLines` admits an unterminated last line and resolves it correctly (probe PR4-2), but every committed JSONL member is built by `jsonl()` with a trailing newline, so `RV-unterminated-tail-dropped-multiline` -- the unterminated last record of a MULTI-LINE member silently dropped -- SURVIVED 2/2. That is a \"never dropped\" violation on a realistic log shape. Add one row with a multi-line member lacking a trailing newline, asserting the last event exists WITH ITS OFFSET.\n7|3. P3-cc. The six per-type `allowed` maps are separate registry sites; `N-body-member` only narrows the shared loop. `RV-allowed-call-live-followup` SURVIVED 2/2 while the class control `RV-allowed-message-escalate` KILLED 2/2. Add one unknown-member row per type that carries an optional member (`live_followup` on a call, `live_attestation` on a result), or state the per-type registry as an explicit bound -- your call, but a census of the six sites must appear in the results either way.\n8|4. P3-dd. `usage_ledger_overflow` overflows `input_tokens` only; `RV-usage-output-arm-widened` SURVIVED 2/2. Add the output-axis row.\n9|5. P3-ee. The clonebundle decode suite pins the duplicate raw_ref but not two distinct unsorted refs: `RV-rawrefs-unsorted-decode` SURVIVED 2/2 while the build side KILLED 2/2. One decode row closes it.\n10|6. P3-ff, P3-gg, P3-hh. State the CRLF behaviour (the trailing CR lies inside the record range and resolves exactly) beside the \"one trailing newline is the terminator\" sentence. Fix matrix section D's `live_followup:null` row: the test is `TestNormalizeNullOptionalBoolsDecodeAsFalse`, not `...AsEmpty`. Drop the unused `\"forty-seven\"` number word from `readme_coverage_pin_test.go`. For `subagent:` with an empty name, either add the row or state the arm as message-only -- `RV-actor-empty-subagent-name` SURVIVED 2/2 and the record refuses one step later at `checkActors`.\n11|7. Re-run the sibling battery TWICE on the final tree with one raw log per plant carrying its subprocess exit, re-run tracecheck, and report the ratio you MEASURE. If a number in the matrix, README, TRACEABILITY or `doc.go` disagrees with what the tools print, the number is wrong, not the tool.\n12|Procedure. Keep the candidate UNCOMMITTED. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept. Run your own package tests and the harnesses BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once (read the command count from the candidate config -- it is not 27). Update the results with a finding-by-finding table (finding -> change -> the test that fails without it -> evidence path), update the conformance matrix and the evidence archive (a real gzip archive, MANIFEST.sha256 with no self-entry, one raw log per plant with its subprocess exit, PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1, zero `__pycache__` anywhere in the tree), re-complete the checklist, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-32ypr2 --role developer` so revision 5 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 2.5 hours in.\n13|PARTIAL-ATTEMPT RECOVERY: if a previous attempt died mid-way, the Story worktree still carries its partial edits -- start with `git -C .temp/STORY-260830-1cyj0q/worktree status --short` and `git diff`, state what you inherited, and verify it against this brief instead of assuming it is correct.\n14|Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The same claude-opus-5 max reviewer contract applies to revision 5.\n15|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n16|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n17|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n18|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n19|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n20|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n21|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n22|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.","final_result":true}}}
